---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const import_ = `@import "@skryensya/core/patterns/scroll-lock.css";`;

const pattern = `html {
  scrollbar-gutter: stable;
}

html:has(dialog:modal) {
  overflow: hidden;
}`;

const nested = `/* The pattern freezes the document. If your app has rails with
   overflow:auto (sidebar, toc), freeze them when there is a modal: */
html:has(dialog:modal) .mi-sidebar,
html:has(dialog:modal) .mi-toc {
  overflow: hidden;
}`;
---

<Base
  title="Scroll lock"
  documentLayout="grid"
  description="Pattern opt-in that freezes the page behind a dialog:modal and reserves the scrollbar gutter so that the layout does not jump."
>
  <h1>Scroll lock</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    When a <code>&lt; dialog&gt; </code> is opened with <code>showModal()</code>, the page behind it has to stop scrolling. Freezing the overflow takes out the classic scrollbar and the content becomes wider by ~15px: that is CLS. This pattern does both things at the same time, and only if you import it.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it is an opt-in pattern</h2>
  <p>
    It is the first system rule that styles the <strong>root of the</strong> document from an opt-in file. Everything else is scoped to its own element. The import <em>is</em> consent: an app that already runs its own scroll lock should not import it, or it is applied twice. That is decision 11 (<code>docs/decisions/0005-dialog-requires-the-native-element.md</code>).
  </p>

  <h2>Importing</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={import_} label="CSS" />

  <h2>What</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={pattern} label="patterns/scroll-lock.css" />
  <ul>
    <li>
      sends <code>scrollbar-gutter: stable</code> in <code>html</code>, always. Reserve the width of the classic scrollbar <em>before</em> freezing, so that the width of the layout is identical with the modal open and closed. Applying it only at the time of the crash is a race that the browser can lose.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>overflow: hidden</code> in <code>html: has(dialog: modal)</code>. Freezes the document behind any <code>&lt; dialog&gt; </code> opened with <code>showModal()</code>: dialog, drawer, vaul, command palette. No JS.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    On systems with scrollbar overlay (the macOS default) the gutter does not take up layout space: the reservation is a no-op and does not bother. In Windows and Linux with a classic scrollbar this is what prevents the jump.
  </p>

  <h2>Try it here</h2>
  <p>
    This site imports the pattern. Open the browser with <kbd class="sk-kbd" data-sk-command-palette-hint>⌘K</kbd> or the navigation drawer in a narrow viewport: the page behind it does not move and the content does not expand. Close and the gutter is still reserved, the same width as always.
  </p>

  <h2>What it does not cover</h2>
  <p>
    <code>showModal()</code> leaves the page inert for pointer and keyboard, but rails with <code>overflow: auto</code> (a sticky sidebar, a toc) can still scroll with the wheel. The pattern does not touch them on purpose: it does not know what rails your app has. If they exist, freeze them with the same <code>: has(dialog: modal)</code>:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={nested} label="CSS" />

  <p>
    Painting the thumb of a region with scroll is another pattern: <a class="sk-link sk-interactive" href="/en/scrollbar">scrollbar</a>. They do not step on each other: one freezes <code>html</code>, the other colors rails.
  </p>

  <h2>Contract</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Opt-in: without the import, the system does not touch <code>html</code>.</li>
    <li>Requires <code>&lt; dialog&gt; </code> + <code>showModal()</code>. A <code>div</code> with <code>role="dialog"</code> does not enter <code>: modal</code> and does not trigger the lock.</li>
    <li>Does not measure or offset against <code>padding-inline-end</code> in JS. The reservation is <code>scrollbar-gutter</code>, platform property.</li>
    <li>It does not replace a scroll lock of your own: if you already have one, do not import this one.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
